<Layout title="About — Javier Ponz" description="Background, experience and current transition of Javier Ponz.">
<section class="hero"><p class="kicker">About</p><h1>People, systems, <span class="serif">service</span>.</h1><p class="lede">My work lives at the boundary: diagnose what is wrong, understand who is affected, explain the trade-offs, and help the system work better next time.</p></section>
<section class="grid"><span class="label">Experience</span><div class="copy"><h2>Apple Retail Spain · 2014—2024</h2><p>I moved through Specialist, Technical Specialist, Genius and In-Store Experience Lead responsibilities. The common thread was not a job title; it was translating technical problems into useful next steps while keeping trust intact.</p><p>As an ISE Lead, I worked with store operations, opening and closing routines, cash handling, events, meetings, resources, maintenance coordination and support for teams when the situation did not fit a neat procedure.</p><p>Apple was a corporate environment with refined protocols. That experience is relevant to service, standards and execution; it is not a claim of startup or engineering experience.</p></div></section>
<section class="grid"><span class="label">Transition</span><div class="copy"><h2>AI and Full Stack</h2><p>Since moving to Aberdeen, I have been retraining in Artificial Intelligence and Full Stack development. I am not presenting myself as a senior software engineer. My depth in backend, infrastructure and production systems is still developing.</p><p>I learn by building, documenting, testing, breaking things and trying again. The goal is useful capability, not the performance of certainty.</p></div></section>
<section class="grid"><span class="label">Principle</span><div class="notice">Leading is serving. Good technical work leaves people with more clarity and more control.</div></section>
</Layout>
